<http://data.silknow.org/object/f0266f29-80fc-31ae-a30a-829553cfe4d2/observation/1>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "The sophisticated ripple moldings on the cabinet’s exterior playfully bring out the lustrous quality of the severe black ebony. Other tropical veneers as well as inlays of mother-of-pearl and green-stained horn enliven the cabinet’s interior which encloses multiple drawers as well as a compartment with a mirror-lined \"stage\" for the display of a treasured artwork. This cabinet is attributed to Herman Doomer, one of the few cabinetmakers working with ebony and mother-of-pearl in Amsterdam, the foremost European market for such exotic materials. Doomer gained fame through the portrait Rembrandt painted of him, also in the Museum's collection (see 29.100.1)."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/f0266f29-80fc-31ae-a30a-829553cfe4d2> .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/f0266f29-80fc-31ae-a30a-829553cfe4d2>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "The sophisticated ripple moldings on the cabinet’s exterior playfully bring out the lustrous quality of the severe black ebony. Other tropical veneers as well as inlays of mother-of-pearl and green-stained horn enliven the cabinet’s interior which encloses multiple drawers as well as a compartment with a mirror-lined \"stage\" for the display of a treasured artwork. This cabinet is attributed to Herman Doomer, one of the few cabinetmakers working with ebony and mother-of-pearl in Amsterdam, the foremost European market for such exotic materials. Doomer gained fame through the portrait Rembrandt painted of him, also in the Museum's collection (see 29.100.1)."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/fb9153e6-16f9-35d5-897a-c88f0712059b> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/5ce84778-73e4-3b11-907c-a2e380df907b> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/ffc83a6f-00e5-39ea-bd2b-c0772f0b1c5e> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/dca59568-2e2a-329b-9cb0-9ea9cb7e2071> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/a959dbfd-9139-3786-b64e-881f46bc8003> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/455f0bf5-8410-301b-a974-b453f52e2304> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/0294ef4c-77cf-3b0d-918b-9c0128b40f6f> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/fa0b4f70-b4ea-3fb1-8e5b-97a344ba534b> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/df04b504-1b95-378e-b9d5-70641b0e6988> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/d75ef679-6da1-30f3-bbfe-185330634899> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/c4aee14e-fb16-31a1-a4fd-676c18f41761> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "The sophisticated ripple moldings on the cabinet’s exterior playfully bring out the lustrous quality of the severe black ebony. Other tropical veneers as well as inlays of mother-of-pearl and green-stained horn enliven the cabinet’s interior which encloses multiple drawers as well as a compartment with a mirror-lined \"stage\" for the display of a treasured artwork. This cabinet is attributed to Herman Doomer, one of the few cabinetmakers working with ebony and mother-of-pearl in Amsterdam, the foremost European market for such exotic materials. Doomer gained fame through the portrait Rembrandt painted of him, also in the Museum's collection (see 29.100.1)."@en ;
